  powerIntro: "Power buttons run immediately, no confirmation — the effects of each are here:",
  powerNotes: "· Sleep = suspend to RAM: wakes almost instantly, but network services stop — this tab shows offline and pages won't load (expected). Wake it with “Wake” (needs MAC + WoL);\n· Shut down: powers off at once; web service stops — afterwards only “Wake” (needs MAC) or the physical button;\n· Restart: reboots at once; the web session drops — heartbeat returns online automatically after reboot;\n· Wake: sends a magic packet to power on; needs MAC and WoL enabled in BIOS.",
  powerHowExec: "How these buttons run (LAN / sol)",
  powerExecNote: "Actions with a “LAN power route” configured send a UDP packet to the route's broadcast addr:port, executed by sol on the target by port — so it works while asleep or with the web service down. Without sol, the packet goes out but nothing runs it.\nActions without a route use the old path: wake sends standard WOL (magic packet); restart/shutdown/sleep call the computer's web API.",
  powerWakeFrom: "Where the wake packet is sent from:",
  powerWakeFromNote: "· Wake route configured → send to the route's address/port (select specific MACs);\n· SSH jump host configured → it broadcasts on the intranet (works from outside);\n· Neither → the phone broadcasts on the LAN (phone must be on the same subnet);\n· Only “remote access” available (public tunnel while away) → wake can't be sent: a magic packet is a LAN UDP broadcast and can't cross a tunnel. Restart/shutdown/sleep call the web API, which works over the tunnel.",
